WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.170
A pupil brought me a torch

2
00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:02.970
that won't light.

3
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.760
New batteries, a good bulb,

4
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.060
and still nothing.

5
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.520
Then we looked closer: one

6
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.780
wire had come loose.

7
00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:15.390
That tiny gap was the whole problem.

8
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.900
Today we find out why.

9
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.940
Today: current, LEDs and resistors, the

10
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.420
circuit inside every connected device.

11
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.980
Electric current is a flow of charge, and

12
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.400
it flows only round a closed loop.

13
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.790
The battery's voltage, in

14
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:36.330
volts, is the push.

15
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.240
The current, in amps or

16
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.780
milliamps, is how much flows.

17
00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.790
Open the loop anywhere, and the

18
00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.150
flow stops everywhere.

19
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.230
Think of water in a pipe.

20
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.000
The pump is the battery, the pipe is the

21
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.820
wire, and the flow of water is the

22
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:56.040
current.

23
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.830
A narrow pipe lets less water

24
00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.610
through: that's resistance.

25
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.220
It's not a perfect picture, but it helps.

26
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.020
Some materials let current

27
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.720
through: they're conductors.

28
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.790
Metals, pencil graphite and

29
00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.610
salty water all conduct.

30
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.670
Plastic, rubber, dry wood and glass

31
00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.870
don't: they're insulators.

32
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.270
That's why a wire is

33
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:23.410
copper inside plastic.

34
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.080
An LED is a light that works

35
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.580
only one way round.

36
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.280
Its long leg goes to plus.

37
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.470
A red LED takes about two volts, and

38
00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.910
wants about ten to twenty milliamps.

39
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.840
Give it much more, and it

40
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.820
burns out in a flash.

41
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:42.670
Quick check!

42
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.850
I connect a red LED straight to a

43
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.930
nine-volt battery, with no resistor.

44
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:49.170
What happens?

45
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.330
It burns out.

46
00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.530
Nothing limits the current,

47
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.610
so far too much flows.

48
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.380
The fix is a resistor: it takes the extra

49
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.360
voltage and holds the current down.

50
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.410
Ohm's law links all three.

51
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.260
Voltage equals current times

52
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.280
resistance: V equals I times R.

53
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.580
Turn it round, and resistance equals

54
00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.340
voltage divided by current.

55
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.960
Let's choose a resistor.

56
00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:21.350
Two AA batteries give three volts.

57
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.080
The red LED takes two, which leaves

58
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.600
one volt for the resistor.

59
00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.790
We want ten milliamps, that's

60
00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.570
zero point zero one amps.

61
00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.910
One divided by zero point zero one

62
00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.930
is one hundred, so a hundred-ohm

63
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.270
resistor is just right.

64
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.600
Now one mistake to avoid: a short circuit.

65
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.990
A wire straight across the

66
00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.710
battery has almost no

67
00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.550
resistance, so a huge current flows.

68
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.110
The wire and the battery get

69
00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.910
hot, and can burn fingers.

70
00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.200
And in class, batteries only:

71
00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.400
mains electricity can kill.

72
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.240
Your turn.

73
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.810
A pupil wants her LED brighter, so

74
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.190
she takes the resistor out.

75
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.500
Good idea?

76
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.010
Not a good idea: without its

77
00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.190
resistor, the LED burns out.

78
00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.840
Use a smaller resistor instead, and

79
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.240
keep under twenty milliamps.

80
00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.710
Remember: a closed loop, the long

81
00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.830
leg to plus, a resistor every time,

82
00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.990
and V equals I times R.

83
00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.570
Try it in class tomorrow:

84
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.070
a conductor hunt.

85
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.520
Two AA batteries, a torch bulb

86
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.320
and three wires are enough.

87
00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.410
Pupils test a coin, a pencil, a plastic

88
00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.290
ruler and a key, and sort them on the

89
00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:45.510
board.

90
00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.480
Next time, we'll make switches decide.
